// THE CONSULTED-GUARD RULE (Ruling 1a, Eric 2026-08-20) — the
// ruling's canonical example, verbatim: a select consults arms
// top-down, structure first, guard second; a consulted guard whose
// current channel is bottom makes the selection UNDECIDABLE and the
// select bottoms; guards of structure-failed or below-the-taken-arm
// arms are irrelevant. "If the programmer has guards, and they
// bottom, it makes the select undecidable when it happens — we have
// to bottom."
// The TRIPLE: arm 1 structure-fails, arm 2 is consulted, its guard
// 3/0 bottoms — undecidable, the select bottoms (empty trace). The
// old engines fell to the wildcard (42) on an invented false.
{let rec f = |a: Array<i64>| -> i64 select a {[x, y] => x + y, [x, y, tail..] if x / y == i64:0 => f(tail), _ => i64:42}; f([i64:3, i64:0, i64:7])}
